# Lesson 6, batch 2 (2026-11-28): top of a strong sequence against a suit contract.
title: A strong sequence against 4♥
id: L06-10
type: exercise
lesson: 6
concepts: 6.3
student: S
board: 10

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  863    984    874     AJ43
  E  T92    AKJT5  KQT     K9
  S  KQJ5   72     A63     T852
  W  A74    Q63    J952    Q76

auction:
  E 1H     = 13+ points, 4+ hearts
  S Pass   = Not enough to bid
  W 2H     = 6-9 points, 3+ hearts
  N Pass   = Nothing to say
  E 4H     = A good hand: game is worth bidding
  S Pass   = Nothing to add
  W Pass   = Done
  N Pass   = Nothing to add

auction recap:
  East opened 1♥ and West raised to 2♥. That was enough for East to bid the
  game. East is declarer, and so you, South, will lead to the first trick.

lead planning:
  declarer: 16-21 = East opened and went straight to game over a simple raise: about 16 or more.
  dummy: 6-9 = West's raise to 2♥ showed 6 to 9 points.
  partner: 0-8 = 40, minus your 10, East's 16 or more and West's 6 to 9, leaves at most 8.
  remind: One of your suits has three touching high cards. What could it do for the defense?

play:
  1   S SK*  W SA  N S3  E S2
      SK key: Top of a sequence. The ♠K gives nothing away, and once the ace is gone your ♠Q and ♠J are winners.
      SK not S5: Right suit! With the king, queen and jack on top, lead the king.
      SK not DA: Cashing the ♦A is safe, but it builds nothing. Which suit has a sequence you could set up?
      SK not D: Leading away from an ace against a suit contract can give declarer a trick with the king. Your ♦A is a sure trick: save it.
      SK not C: A club is a guess. Which of your suits has a sequence on top?
      SK not H: Trumps can wait. Which of your suits has a sequence on top?
      note: Dummy's ace wins. Your ♠Q and ♠J are now set up, ready for when you get in.
  2   W H3  N H4  E HA  S H2
      note: Declarer draws trumps.
  3   E HK  S H7  W H6  N H8
  4   E H5  S C2  W HQ  N H9
      note: Out of trumps, you throw a small club.
  5   W D2  N D4  E DK  S DA
      note: Declarer goes after diamonds. You take your ♦A at once: if you wait, dummy's fourth diamond will give declarer a discard.
  6   S SQ  W S4  N S6  E S9
      note: Now cash your spades.
  7   S SJ  W S7  N S8  E ST
      note: The ♠J is good too.
  8   S C5  W C6  N CA  E C9
      note: Partner's ♣A is the fourth trick: down one.
  9   N C4  E CK  S C8  W C7
      note: The rest are declarer's.
  10  E DQ  S D3  W D5  N D7
  11  E DT  S D6  W DJ  N D8
  12  W D9  N CJ  E HT  S S5
  13  E HJ  S CT  W CQ  N C3

play recap:
  Your ♠K pushed out dummy's ace at trick one, so your ♠Q and ♠J were ready
  the moment the ♦A got you in. Declarer had two spades, a diamond and a
  club to lose, and no time to get rid of any of them. Lead a club or a
  diamond instead, and declarer draws trumps and sets up dummy's diamonds:
  the fourth one takes care of a spade loser, and 4♥ makes. That is what a
  sequence lead is for: it builds tricks quickly and gives nothing away.
